  -DBENCH=0 -DTESTS=0 /app`). Callers reach it in two ways:

  * by compiling a small standalone C program that does `#include "relic.h"`
    (resolved through `-I/app/include -I/build/include`) and linking it against
    the built library objects, then executing the binary with
    `LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/build/lib`. The link step is written three ways:
        /usr/bin/gcc -o exe probe.c -I/app/include -I/build/include /build/lib/librelic.so -lm
        /usr/bin/gcc -o exe probe.c -I/app/include -I/build/include -static /build/lib/librelic_s.a -lm
        gcc -o exe probe.c -I/app/include -I/build/include -L/build/lib -lrelic -lm
  * by reading the raw bytes of /build/lib/librelic.so and
    /build/lib/librelic_s.a directly.

  Caller working directories are temporary; every library, header, and toolchain
  path above is absolute. The runtime is Linux with the distribution toolchain at
  /usr/bin/gcc and /usr/bin/ld.

