This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  A CommonJS module under Node (node:18 image; binary /usr/local/bin/node), loaded
  by require() from the driver script /tests/tarfs_driver.js through two module
  requests:
      mod = require(request);                        // request is '"'"'tar-fs'"'"' or '"'"'/app'"'"'
      emit('"'"'RESOLVED'"'"', fs.realpathSync(require.resolve(request)));
  - request '"'"'tar-fs'"'"' resolves through NODE_PATH=/app/node_modules to
    /app/node_modules/tar-fs/index.js
  - request '"'"'/app'"'"' resolves through "main": "index.js" in /app/package.json to
    /app/index.js
  The caller takes the realpath of require.resolve() and requires it to be inside
  the application tree /app, and both module requests are driven, so the
  implementation must be present at both paths. Both files are project-owned and
  staged by the image build (the package source at /app/index.js is also copied to
  /app/node_modules/tar-fs/). Resolution is independent of the caller'"'"'s working
  directory: the driver is invoked by absolute path and the bare request resolves
  through NODE_PATH. Runtime dependencies required() by the module — tar-stream,
  pump, mkdirp, chownr — are preexisting packages installed under
  /app/node_modules by the image build.

## Must provide
  exports.pack      function on module.exports; called with one argument, a
                    directory path string; what it returns is piped by the caller
                    into a file write stream and watched for stream '"'"'finish'"'"' /
                    '"'"'error'"'"' events
  exports.extract   function on module.exports; called with one argument, a
                    directory path string; the caller pipes a readable
                    tar-archive byte stream into what it returns and watches it
                    for stream '"'"'finish'"'"' / '"'"'error'"'"' events
  /app/index.js     module source resolved by require('"'"'/app'"'"'); /app/package.json
                    declares name "tar-fs" and "main": "index.js"
  /app/node_modules/tar-fs/index.js
                    the copy resolved by require('"'"'tar-fs'"'"') under
                    NODE_PATH=/app/node_modules, with the package.json alongside
                    it carrying the same identity

## Called as
  The driver is launched by the caller as a subprocess:
      subprocess.run(["/usr/local/bin/node", "/tests/tarfs_driver.js"] + args, ...)
  with args one of:
      ["extract", <request>, <tarball>, <extract-dir>]
      ["pack",    <request>, <src-dir>, <out-tarball>]
  <request>     2nd positional argument; a string, either '"'"'tar-fs'"'"' or '"'"'/app'"'"'.
                Both values are used across the call sites.
  <tarball>     3rd positional argument; a string, a path to a tar archive file
                (USTAR format, authored by the caller). Entries the caller authors
                carry: regular file entries (name string + byte content),
                directory entries (name string), symlink entries (name string +
                linkname string), and hardlink entries, tar type '"'"'link'"'"' (name
                string + linkname string). An archive may contain a regular file
                entry whose name is the same as an earlier entry'"'"'s name.
  <extract-dir> 4th positional argument; a string, a path to a directory.
  <src-dir>     3rd positional argument; a string, a path to a directory tree
                (may be nested, containing regular files).
  <out-tarball> 4th positional argument; a string, a path to an archive file.
  Inside the driver the library is reached as:
      fs.createReadStream(tarball).pipe(mod.extract(extractDir))
      mod.pack(srcDir).pipe(fs.createWriteStream(outTarball))
  i.e. extract() and pack() are each called with exactly one argument (the
  directory path); no options object is passed on either call.

## Setup required
  - NODE_PATH=/app/node_modules in the environment, so require('"'"'tar-fs'"'"') resolves
    for a script that lives outside /app (the driver lives in /tests).
  - /app/package.json must declare "main": "index.js" with package name "tar-fs"
    so require('"'"'/app'"'"') lands on the module source.
  - Both copies, /app/index.js and /app/node_modules/tar-fs/index.js, must be the
    implementation: callers resolve and drive each of them and check the resolved
    realpath is inside /app before trusting a run.
  - Runtime dependencies (tar-stream, pump, mkdirp, chownr) already installed
    under /app/node_modules by the image build.

## Test harness boundary
  A Python pytest runner launches the driver through subprocess and parses the
  driver'"'"'s tab-separated stdout protocol (NODE_OPTIONS, RESOLVED, RESULT lines).
  The driver only performs the requested operation and prints those facts; the
  runner itself authors the tar archives (Python tarfile, USTAR), creates the
  extraction directories and the source trees, and makes its checks by reading
  the filesystem directly from the Python process — the driver'"'"'s stdout is never
  the record of what packing or extraction did. The runner also inspects the
  reported NODE_OPTIONS line and the resolved realpath before trusting a run.
  The driver script, the Python runner, and the temporary tarballs, extraction
  directories, and source trees they create belong to the calling side; none of
  them is part of the implementation and none should be rebuilt.
