## Reached at
  A CommonJS module loaded by absolute path under the image'"'"'s Node runtime
  (/usr/local/bin/node, node:18), working directory /app:
      const EtsyClient = require('"'"'/app/src/EtsyClient.js'"'"');
  The module is staged at that path by the image build. The package manifest
  /app/package.json (name node-etsy-client) declares it as the package main
  (./src/EtsyClient.js), so require('"'"'node-etsy-client'"'"') resolved from /app
  lands on the same file. Callers also resolve the load with require.resolve,
  realpath it, and require the real path to lie inside /app — so the module
  must genuinely live at /app/src/EtsyClient.js; a copy elsewhere, a system
  package, or an empty stub cannot serve these calls.

## Must provide
  /app/src/EtsyClient.js   CommonJS module; module.exports is the EtsyClient class
  new EtsyClient(options)  constructor; takes a single plain-object argument
  findAllShops(options)                        instance method, returns a Promise
  getShop(options)                             instance method, returns a Promise
  findAllShopSections(listingId, options)      instance method, returns a Promise
  findAllShopListingsActive(options)           instance method, returns a Promise
  getListing(listingId, options)               instance method, returns a Promise
  getVariationImages(listingId, options)       instance method, returns a Promise
  findAllListingImages(listingId, options)     instance method, returns a Promise
  getInventory(listingId, options)             instance method, returns a Promise
  getAttributes(listingId, options)            instance method, returns a Promise
  getProduct(listingId, productId, options)    instance method, returns a Promise
  findAllListingShippingProfileEntries(listingId, options)   instance method, returns a Promise
  etsyApiFetch(endpoint, options)              instance method, the request dispatcher, itself a public member of the class; returns a Promise
  getOptions(options)                          instance method, called synchronously

## Called as
  new EtsyClient(options)
      options — 1st argument; a plain object (callers also pass an empty one).
      Keys callers use: apiKey, apiUrl, shop, lang — each a string when present.
      Shapes passed: { apiKey: <string> }, { apiKey: <string>, lang: <string> },
      { apiKey: <string>, shop: <string> }, { apiKey: <string>, apiUrl: <string> },
      { apiKey: <string>, apiUrl: <string>, shop: <string> }, and {}.
      apiUrl values seen include http URLs on 127.0.0.1 with an explicit port
      and https URLs. The same settings may alternatively arrive through the
      environment variables ETSY_API_KEY, ETSY_API_ENDPOINT, ETSY_SHOP, and
      ETSY_LANG.
  client.findAllShops()                       no arguments; a trailing options object is optional
  client.getShop()                            no arguments; shop is supplied via the constructor options
  client.findAllShopSections(12345)           listingId — 1st positional argument; a number
  client.findAllShopListingsActive()          no arguments
  client.getListing(12345)                    listingId — 1st positional argument; a number
  client.getVariationImages(12345)            listingId — 1st positional argument; a number
  client.findAllListingImages(12345)          listingId — 1st positional argument; a number
  client.getInventory(12345)                  listingId — 1st positional argument; a number
  client.getAttributes(12345)                 listingId — 1st positional argument; a number
  client.getProduct(12345, 777)               listingId — 1st positional argument; productId — 2nd positional argument; both numbers
  client.findAllListingShippingProfileEntries(12345)   listingId — 1st positional argument; a number
  client.etsyApiFetch('"'"'/shops'"'"')               endpoint — 1st positional argument; a string URL path (callers pass values such as '"'"'/shops'"'"'); a trailing options object is optional
  client.getOptions({})                       options — 1st argument; a plain object; callers
  client.getOptions({ limit: 10, offset: 5 }) also pass objects carrying arbitrary query-parameter keys with numeric values

  Every Promise-returning entry point above is awaited by the caller. Each one
  is exercised both with an apiUrl pointing at a local server the caller runs
  and with apiUrl values pointing at endpoints nothing answers, and the caller
  reads whichever value the promise settles with in each case. Both driving
  suites travel these same entry points to reach the dispatcher.

## Setup required
  Callers pin the working directory to /app and start the interpreter with an
  environment containing only PATH, so no ETSY_* variables are set during the
  calls and configuration arrives through the constructor options object.
  The module'"'"'s top-level require('"'"'query-string'"'"') and require("node-fetch")
  must resolve from /app/node_modules (query-string ^6.13.7 and node-fetch
  ^2.6.1 per the manifest, installed by the image build). These are
  pre-existing third-party dependencies, not part of the code being
  reconstructed.
  No project-owned service runs: the library is loaded in-process, and every
  base URL is supplied per call through the apiUrl constructor option.

## Test harness boundary
  A Python driver under pytest reaches the library by launching the Node
  interpreter through subprocess with the program fed on stdin, the working
  directory pinned to /app, and a scrubbed environment. The embedded program
  requires the module by the absolute path above, starts an ephemeral local
  HTTP server (http.createServer on 127.0.0.1, random port), hands that
  server'"'"'s URL to the client as apiUrl, records the request URLs that arrive,
  drives each entry point, and prints a single JSON report line on stdout that
  the driver parses.
  The driver, the embedded program, the ephemeral local server, and the report
  protocol all belong to the calling side. They are not part of the
  implementation and must not be rebuilt or altered.
```

