## Reached at
  An executable built from the source tree at /app and installed under
  /usr/local. /app is the Git v2.26.1 source, staged there by the image
  build. Every caller invokes the dispatcher by absolute path:

      subprocess.run(["/usr/local/bin/git", <command>, ...],
                     env=<fully replaced environment>,
                     cwd=<repository directory>, input=<stdin text>)

  Helper programs resolve through the git exec path
  (/usr/local/libexec/git-core) when callers run `git credential-store` or
  `git remote-http`.

  A caller-side prelude regenerates the installed binaries from the tree
  before each run:

      make -C /app prefix=/usr/local -j$(nproc)
      make -C /app prefix=/usr/local install

  and records the build outcome in a status file (with build and install
  logs alongside); every caller reads that file and requires it to report
  the build as OK before it invokes git at all. The callers therefore
  execute exactly what /app builds, so the source tree under /app is the
  code being reconstructed. The URL handling reached through the credential
  commands and the submodule-URL handling reached through `git fsck` live in
  credential.c and fsck.c within that tree.

## Must provide
  /usr/local/bin/git        dispatcher executable, product of the two make
                            invocations above
  git credential fill       subcommand; reads a credential-protocol block
                            from stdin
  git credential approve    subcommand; same stdin protocol
  git credential reject     subcommand; same stdin protocol
  git credential-store      helper program on the git exec path; takes
                            `--file=<path>` plus a `store` or `erase`
                            argument; reads the credential protocol from
                            stdin
  git remote-http           helper program on the git exec path; takes one
                            URL argument
  git push                  subcommand; called with a destination path and a
                            refspec
  git fsck                  subcommand; called with no arguments inside a
                            repository
  init, config, add, commit, rev-parse, --version
                            standard plumbing used by caller-side setup,
                            resolved through the same dispatcher and build

## Called as
  git credential fill | approve | reject
      ["/usr/local/bin/git", "credential", "fill"]   (also "approve",
      "reject")
      stdin is a text block of `key=value` lines terminated by an empty
      line; keys seen at the callers: url, username, password. Each value is
      a string:
          url=<string>\n
          username=<string>\n
          password=<string>\n
          \n

  git credential-store
      ["/usr/local/bin/git", "credential-store", "--file=<path>", "store"]
      (also "erase" in place of "store"; `--file=<path>` is a file path
      string)
      stdin, in both block shapes seen at the callers:
          protocol=<string>\n host=<string>\n username=<string>\n
          password=<string>\n \n
          url=<string>\n username=<string>\n password=<string>\n \n

  git remote-http
      ["/usr/local/bin/git", "remote-http", "<url>"]
      <url> — argv[2]; a string. stdin is fed one empty line, which
      terminates the command stream.

  git push
      ["/usr/local/bin/git", "push", "-q", "<dst>", "HEAD"]
      run with cwd inside the source repository; <dst> — a directory path
      to a bare destination repository; "HEAD" — a refspec string. The
      source repository carries a committed `.gitmodules` of this shape:
          [submodule "<name>"]
          \turl = <string>
          \tpath = <string>
      and the destination repository has its `transfer.fsckObjects` setting
      enabled, which routes incoming objects through the fsck machinery.

  git fsck
      ["/usr/local/bin/git", "fsck"]
      run with cwd inside a repository whose HEAD commit carries a
      `.gitmodules` of the same shape.

## Setup required
  - The reconstruction lives in the /app source tree and must keep it
    buildable under exactly the two make invocations shown above: the
    prelude runs them before each suite, and every caller gates on the
    recorded build status before invoking git. A binary replaced or wrapped
    outside the tree is overwritten by the prelude and is never what the
    callers execute.
  - Callers replace the entire process environment; the values that reach a
    call are:
        PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
        HOME=<a fresh empty directory>
        GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1
        GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0
        GIT_ASKPASS=true
        LANG=C
        LC_ALL=C
    No system or global git configuration participates.
  - For `credential fill`, `approve`, and `reject`, callers first set the
    repository config `credential.helper` to `store --file=<path>`; the
    credential machinery dispatches to the helper through that setting, so
    helper dispatch must resolve `store` to the installed
    `git-credential-store` program.
  - Commands run with cwd inside per-invocation temporary repositories; all
    repository state the callers use is created in those directories.

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest runner (a uv-managed CPython 3.11 venv) drives every entry point
  through `subprocess.run` against the absolute binary path, with a fully
  replaced environment. A shell prelude ahead of the suites rebuilds and
  reinstalls git from /app and writes the build status file and the build
  and install logs. The temporary HOME directories, repositories,
  credential-store files, and `.gitmodules` contents are caller-side
  fixtures. None of this belongs to the implementation; the engineer
  reconstructs only the git source tree under /app.
